1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,423
1957. Florence, Oregon.

2
00:00:04,603 --> 00:00:09,810
A journalist flies over coastal dunes that swallow highways, forests, and houses.

3
00:00:09,990 --> 00:00:15,349
He came to write an article about the men trying to stop them… with grass.

4
00:00:15,999 --> 00:00:17,131
He never finished it.

5
00:00:18,131 --> 00:00:26,433
His notes kept growing until they became something else: one of the best-selling science fiction novels in history.

6
00:00:26,613 --> 00:00:33,103
Nearly 70 years later, cinemas are preparing for the chapter his own readers didn't want to see.

7
00:00:33,653 --> 00:00:35,917
This is the story of Dune.

8
00:00:36,717 --> 00:00:40,415
CHAPTER 1 — THE ARTICLE HE NEVER FINISHED

9
00:00:40,715 --> 00:00:50,149
Frank Herbert was a journalist and short-story writer. In Oregon, he saw something that wouldn't let him go: the sand moved like a very slow sea.

10
00:00:50,329 --> 00:00:57,424
He began reading about deserts, ecology, religion, and power. He spent six years writing.

11
00:00:57,604 --> 00:01:02,962
Out of that came a desert planet, where water is worth more than any treasure.

12
00:01:03,142 --> 00:01:11,746
The story came out first as a serial in a science fiction magazine, between 1963 and 1965.

13
00:01:12,396 --> 00:01:16,245
The hardest part was still ahead: turning it into a book.

14
00:01:17,045 --> 00:01:20,743
CHAPTER 2 — MORE THAN TWENTY REJECTIONS

15
00:01:21,043 --> 00:01:29,119
More than twenty publishers rejected it. It was too long. Too complicated. Too strange.

16
00:01:29,299 --> 00:01:36,318
Finally, an unexpected publisher said yes: Chilton, famous for its auto repair manuals.

17
00:01:36,498 --> 00:01:42,686
His editor, Sterling Lanier, had read the magazine serial and bet $7,500 on it.

18
00:01:42,866 --> 00:01:48,753
The book came out in August 1965. It was expensive and sold poorly.

19
00:01:49,453 --> 00:01:51,264
Lanier was fired.

20
00:01:52,114 --> 00:01:58,907
But the following year, it won the two biggest awards in science fiction. And it began passing from hand to hand.

21
00:01:59,707 --> 00:02:03,556
CHAPTER 3 — THE HERO WAS A WARNING

22
00:02:03,856 --> 00:02:08,535
By the late sixties, Dune was already a cult book among young readers.

23
00:02:08,715 --> 00:02:17,998
But Herbert had something to say about his own protagonist. In 1969, he published the sequel: Dune Messiah.

24
00:02:18,178 --> 00:02:25,574
The editor who had published the first story rejected it. He said readers wanted heroes, not antiheroes.

25
00:02:25,754 --> 00:02:32,396
For Herbert, that was the entire point: a charismatic leader can be the greatest danger of all.

26
00:02:33,046 --> 00:02:36,668
Remember that book. It is the one arriving in theaters in December.

27
00:02:37,668 --> 00:02:42,952
Herbert wrote six Dune novels. He died in 1986.

28
00:02:43,132 --> 00:02:53,245
In 2009, astronomers began naming the plains of Titan, Saturn's moon, after the planets of Dune. Today, there is a real Arrakis.

29
00:02:54,045 --> 00:02:58,422
CHAPTER 4 — THE GREATEST MOVIE NEVER MADE

30
00:02:58,722 --> 00:03:07,250
In the mid-seventies, Chilean director Alejandro Jodorowsky wanted to bring Dune to the big screen. And he wanted to do it on a massive scale.

31
00:03:07,430 --> 00:03:12,562
Comic artist Moebius drew some 3,000 storyboards to plan every single shot.

32
00:03:12,742 --> 00:03:22,403
He recruited Swiss artist H. R. Giger, Pink Floyd for the music, and Salvador Dalí as the Emperor. Dalí asked for $100,000 an hour.

33
00:03:22,583 --> 00:03:28,621
The script would have run for more than ten hours. No Hollywood studio was willing to pay for it.

34
00:03:28,801 --> 00:03:32,725
They spent $2 million, and the project collapsed.

35
00:03:33,475 --> 00:03:38,834
But part of that team stayed together. A few years later, they reunited to make Alien.

36
00:03:39,634 --> 00:03:43,105
CHAPTER 5 — THE DESERT IN MEXICO

37
00:03:43,405 --> 00:03:52,160
In 1984, the first movie finally arrived. Directed by David Lynch, it was filmed in studios in Mexico City.

38
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:58,227
It cost around $40 million, and in the United States it grossed less than $31 million.

39
00:03:58,727 --> 00:04:03,406
Critics tore it apart. Lynch ended up disowning it.

40
00:04:03,586 --> 00:04:11,360
But on opening day, right at the front of the line, stood a 17-year-old Canadian kid who had read the book at 13.

41
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:15,691
He walked out thinking that someday, someone would try again.

42
00:04:16,441 --> 00:04:18,931
That someone was going to be him.

43
00:04:19,731 --> 00:04:23,203
CHAPTER 6 — THE THIRTEEN-YEAR-OLD BOY

44
00:04:23,503 --> 00:04:32,031
In 2016, at the Venice Film Festival, Denis Villeneuve was asked what his dream project was. He answered: Dune.

45
00:04:32,211 --> 00:04:37,192
A production company that had just bought the rights read the interview and called him.

46
00:04:37,372 --> 00:04:44,693
During pre-production, inside his father's old desk, they found the Dune storyboards he had drawn when he was 13.

47
00:04:44,873 --> 00:04:50,458
He filmed in the deserts of Jordan and Abu Dhabi, and split the novel into two movies.

48
00:04:50,638 --> 00:04:59,091
The pandemic delayed the release by nearly a year. The studio released it in theaters and on streaming the same day. Villeneuve protested.

49
00:04:59,741 --> 00:05:08,118
Even so, it grossed over $400 million. And it won 6 Oscars, more than any other film that night.

50
00:05:08,918 --> 00:05:11,560
CHAPTER 7 — THE SECOND HALF

51
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:17,746
In March 2024, Part Two arrived. This time, exclusively in theaters.

52
00:05:17,926 --> 00:05:23,964
It grossed over $700 million, nearly $300 million more than the first.

53
00:05:24,144 --> 00:05:30,031
And the 1965 novel had now sold nearly 20 million copies.

54
00:05:30,211 --> 00:05:36,324
Notes for an unfinished magazine article had turned into one of the biggest sagas in cinema.

55
00:05:37,074 --> 00:05:39,036
But the uncomfortable part was still missing.

56
00:05:39,836 --> 00:05:42,402
CHAPTER 8 — DUNESDAY

57
00:05:42,702 --> 00:05:48,967
The third movie adapts Dune Messiah: the book that original editor refused to publish.

58
00:05:49,147 --> 00:05:52,543
Villeneuve said it will be his final Dune film.

59
00:05:52,723 --> 00:06:00,195
It opens in mid-December. In the United States, on the 18th… the exact same day as Avengers: Doomsday.

60
00:06:00,745 --> 00:06:04,216
The internet already named that day: Dunesday.

61
00:06:04,396 --> 00:06:08,094
And Dune locked down the IMAX screens for its premiere.

62
00:06:08,744 --> 00:06:14,556
In 1957, the men of Florence managed to stop the sand with grass.

63
00:06:15,456 --> 00:06:18,852
Herbert's sand, however, never stopped moving.
